    <briefdescription>
<para>Requirements are checks that stop the test case when they fail. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para><computeroutput>t.require (condition)</computeroutput> checks a condition as <computeroutput>expect()</computeroutput> does, and also stops the test case when it fails. Use it when the rest of the test case depends on the condition, for example to check that a pointer is not null before using it.</para>
<para>A failed requirement is reported and counted like a failed expectation. What follows depends on the build:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>with exceptions, the rest of the test case is skipped, and the run continues with the next test case; this is the behaviour of the fatal assertions of most C++ test frameworks (<computeroutput>ASSERT_*</computeroutput> in GoogleTest, <computeroutput>REQUIRE()</computeroutput> in Catch2 and doctest, <computeroutput>BOOST_REQUIRE()</computeroutput> in Boost.Test);</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>without exceptions, a test case cannot be stopped alone, so the whole test run is aborted with <computeroutput>runner::abort()</computeroutput>, as Catch2 does: the results of the test case are written, ending with the failed requirement (followed, with the TAP reporter, by a <computeroutput>Bail out!</computeroutput> line), the remaining test cases do not run, and no totals are written. The process is then terminated with <computeroutput>::abort()</computeroutput>, unless the application set another handler with <computeroutput>runner::set_abort_handler()</computeroutput>.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><simplesect kind="par"><title>Example</title><para></para>
</simplesect>
<programlisting filename=".cpp"><codeline><highlight class="normal">ts.test<sp/>(</highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;Check<sp/>the<sp/>buffer&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">,<sp/>[]<sp/>(</highlight><highlight class="keyword">auto</highlight><highlight class="normal">&amp;<sp/>t)<sp/>{</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/></highlight><highlight class="keyword">const</highlight><highlight class="normal"><sp/></highlight><highlight class="keywordtype">char</highlight><highlight class="normal">*<sp/>buffer<sp/>=<sp/>get_buffer<sp/>();</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/>t.require<sp/>(mt::ne<sp/>(buffer,<sp/></highlight><highlight class="keyword">nullptr</highlight><highlight class="normal">))<sp/>&lt;&lt;<sp/></highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;buffer<sp/>allocated&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">;</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/>t.expect<sp/>(mt::eq<sp/>(buffer[0],<sp/></highlight><highlight class="charliteral">&apos;a&apos;</highlight><highlight class="normal">));</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">});</highlight></codeline>
</programlisting>  </para>
    </detaileddescription>
